## 1. Introduction
Carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ and methane $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)$ are major greenhouse gases. In the past decades, the level of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the atmosphere has significantly increased. Capture of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from their emission sources is important to control their emissions into the atmosphere. In some cases, separation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is also important in their utilization [1–3]. The coexistence of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in natural gas processing can produce pipeline problem. Landfill gas also contains mainly ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Thus, separation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is critical for the application of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have been increasingly attracted as novel microporous materials in applications of adsorption, separation, and catalysis [4–8]. It has been generally believed that MOFs are formed due to the coordination bonds between metal salt and multidentate ligands. This reaction relies on the reagents and the synthesis parameters such as solvent composition, temperature, pressure, additives, and pH [9,10]. In the past decades, a lot of MOFs have been designed and synthesized; however, most of MOFs suffer from low hydrothermal stability.
Recently, a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF (UiO-66) was synthesized by Cavka et al. [11] and it exhibits high hydrothermal stability up to $773\mathrm{K}.$ Several other researchers have further investigated the modification and functionalization of UiO-66 [12,13]. Chavan et al. [12] reported an insertion of (arene) $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$ species in UiO-66 and its photoinduced reactivity. Kandiah et al. [13] reported a synthesis of tagged UiO-66 using three different linker ligands, $H_{2}N-H_{2}B D C$ $0_{2}\mathsf{N-H_{2}B D C}$ , and $B\Gamma{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ . The results demonstrated that incorporating active functional groups into the UiO-66 structure would not affect thermal and chemical stability. However, few investigations of UiO-66 in gas adsorption have been reported.
We have investigated the synthesis of UiO-66 using solvent exchange activation process to enhance porous structure and its application in $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption [14]. In this paper, we report the synthesis of Zr-MOF with addition of ammonium hydroxide in synthesis solution and the effects of the additive on the structure and adsorption behavior in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption.
## 2. Experimentals
## 2.1. Synthesis of Zr-MOFs
All chemicals including DMF (Dimethylformamide $99\%$ , BDC (Benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid, $98.9\%$ ), chloroform $(99\%)$ , $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ $(24\%)$ , and $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{Cl}_{4}}$ were supplied by Sigma–Aldrich, Thermo Fisher Scientific, and Perth Scientific and were used without further purification.
Zr-MOF was synthesized following the procedure reported by Cavka et al. [11] with some amendments. Generally, $2.27\:\mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ , $2.27\:\mathrm{mmol}$ of BDC were mixed together in $405.38\mathrm{mmol}$ of DMF for $30\mathrm{min}$ , then the homogenous mixture was placed inside a Parr PTFE-lined digestion vessel of $45~\mathrm{mL}$ . The autoclave was placed in an oven preheated to $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
In preparation of $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ modified $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF, the solvent (DMF) was divided into two parts at 202.69 mmol each. In the first solution, BDC was added in and stirred for $15\mathrm{min}$ , then $0.4\:\mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ was added in dropwise under stirring. The second DMF solution was mixed with $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{zr}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ for stirring about $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then the two solutions were mixed together and stirred for $20\mathrm{min}$ .
The resulted solution was placed in the autoclave at 393 K for $24\mathrm{h}$ This $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF was referred as Zr-MOF-NH4.
After synthesis, the above crystalline materials were immersed inside chloroform solution for $5{\mathsf{d}}$ and then the solids were filtered and dried in vacuum. Finally, these materials were heated in an oven at 463 K overnight.
## 2.2. Characterizations of Zr-MOFs
Thermal stability of Zr-MOFs was checked by a thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) instrument (TGA/DSC1 STARe system-METTLER TOLEDO). Zr-MOF samples were loaded into a pan and heated to $1173\mathrm{K}$ at a rate of $5\mathrm{K/min}$ . The air gas flow rate was maintained at $10\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ . FTIR spectra (Spectrum 100-FT-IR Spectrometer, Perkin-Elmer) were obtained to check the stability of the functional groups on the organic ligands. The spectrum was scanned from 600 to $4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with a resolution of $4\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ by using an attenuated total reflectance (ATR) technique.
SEM analysis (Zeiss NEON 40 EsB CrossBeam) was used to capture and determine the morphologies of the crystalline Zr-MOF samples. X-ray powder diffraction patterns were obtained with an $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffractometer (D8 Advance-Bruker aXS) using Cu Ka radiation $\mathit{\Omega}_{\lambda}=1.5406\mathrm{\AA}$ ), with accelerating voltage and current of $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40~\mathrm{{mA}}$ , respectively. A Quantachrome instrument (Autosorb-1) was used to determine ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms as well as the pore size and surface area of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs. The samples were evacuated at $473\mathrm{K}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ prior to the adsorption measurements under high vacuum. Surface area was determined by the BET method, and pore size distribution was derived from the BJH method.
## 2.3. $C H_{4}$ and $C O_{2}$ adsorption tests on Zr-MOFs
$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption tests were conducted on a Micromeritics instrument (ASAP 2050) at varying pressure up to $1000\mathrm{kPa}$ and $273\mathrm{K}.$ In each run, $0.3{\mathrm{g}}$ sample was degassed at a ramp rate of $1\mathrm{K/min}$ to $473\mathrm{K}$ and holding at this temperature for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
XRD analysis was first carried out to investigate crystallite structure as shown in Fig. 1. The XRD profiles of Zr-MOF and ZrMOF-NH4 show similar patterns, which are the same of UiO-66 as reported before [11]. This suggests that additive of $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ will not cause significant change in crystallite structure of Zr-MOF. Based on the Scherrer equation, particle size of two samples was obtained. The crystallite sizes for Zr-MOF and Zr-MOF-NH4 are 63 and $41\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, which suggests that addition of $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ could result in a reduced particle size.
Crystal size of Zr-MOF and Zr-MOF-NH4 was then checked by SEM and the images are shown in Fig. 2. It is seen that crystallite size of Zr-MOF was similar with cubic shape at about $100\mathrm{nm}$ but the particle size of Zr-MOF-NH4 was reduced, which confirm the results from XRD analysis. Addition of ammonium hydroxide in synthesis solution will induce two effects. Firstly, addition of ammonium hydroxide will increase pH, leading to an increase in BDC solubility. Secondly, the metal ion will not aggregate to form larger crystals.
Fig. 3 displays $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms and pore size distributions of Zr-MOF samples. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption on both Zr-MOF samples presents as microporous structure. The textural properties are given in Table 1. Zr-MOF has BET surface area and pore volume of $1433\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.63~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, higher than the ones reported by Cavka et al. [11]. This is due to different activation process used in this investigation. In this work, solvent exchange by chloroform for DMF was employed before heat treatment. Using chloroform in activation can effectively remove DMF and BDC residuals, resulting in higher surface area and pore volume. Compared with Zr-MOF, Zr-MOF-NH4 shows a bit lower BET surface area and pore volume. However, the average pore sizes of two samples are similar with slightly high value for Zr-MOF-NH4, which is probably due to reduced micropores.
Pore size distributions from the BJH method of two Zr-MOF samples also show some differences. For $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF, two peaks centered at 1.7 and $5.2\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, were observed. However, $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOF-NH4 presents a shift of pore size with two peaks. One is centered at $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ , same as Zr-MOF, the other one is at $3.4\mathrm{nm}$ . The results suggest that $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ could also result in a change in mesoporous structure of Zr-MOF.
Fig. 4 illustrates FTIR spectra of Zr-MOF samples. As seen, ZrMOF and Zr-MOF-NH4 present similar profiles, suggesting the similar surface functionality. It can be recognized that a broad IR band at the region of $3175{-}3600\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ occurred due to intercrystalline water and physisorbed water. Two sharp peaks at 1584 and $1393\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated to the in- and out-of-phase stretching modes of carboxylates [11]. The peaks at 676, 702, 728, and 745 are due to OH and CH bending. No functional groups related to ${\sf N}{\mathrm{-}}{\sf H}$ were found on Zr-MOF-NH4.
Thermal stability analysis of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOF and Zr-MOF-NH4 was conducted using a TGA. Weight loss profiles and weight loss rate are presented in Fig. 5. It is clear that three-stage weight loss occurred on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs. The first weight loss appearing before $373\mathrm{K}$ is due to the evaporation of surface adsorbed water on Zr-MOFs. The second weight loss at 473–593 K is attributed to the decomposition of DMF, which was not exchanged by chloroform. The third stage of weight loss at $723-833\mathrm{K}$ is ascribed to the decomposition of ZrMOF to $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ . Compared with $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF, Zr-MOF-NH4 exhibited less weight loss rate in the first and second stages, suggesting more thermal stability of Zr-MOF-NH4. Previous investigations showed that functionality of Zr-MOF (UiO-66) with Cr, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , and Br would not change the thermal stability [12,13].
The adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF and ZrMOF-NH4 at $273\mathrm{K}$ are presented in Fig. 6. In general, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption will increase with increasing pressure and no equilibrium can be reached. Zr-MOF samples show much higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption. For $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption, ZrMOF will show higher adsorption than that of Zr-MOF-NH4. At pressure of $988\mathrm{kPa}$ , ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption on Zr-MOF and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-NH4 is 8.1 and $7.8\:\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , respectively, while $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption on ZrMOF and Zr-MOF-NH4 is 3.6 and $2.8\:\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , respectively. It has been believed that physisorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ relies on the porosity of materials. Table 1 shows that $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF has higher BET surface area and pore volume than those of Zr-MOF-NH4, which results in higher adsorption capacity on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF.
The isotherms were fitted by the Freundlich isotherm model as shown below.
$$
Q_{\mathrm{e}}=K_{\mathrm{F}}P_{\mathrm{e}}^{1/n}
$$
where $Q_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $P_{\mathrm{e}}$ are equilibrium adsorption and pressure, respectively. $K_{\mathrm{F}}$ and $n$ are the Freundlich adsorption isotherm constants, being indicative of the extent of the adsorption and the degree of nonlinearity between gas pressure and adsorption, respectively. Table 2 presents the parameters obtained from the model fitting and it is seen that the Freundlich isotherm produced excellent fitting to the data.
In the past a few years, many types of MOFs have been tested for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption. Millward and Yaghi [15] investigated ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ isotherms for MOF-2, MOF-505, $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ , MOF-74, IRMOF-11, IRMOF-3, IRMOF-6, and IRMOF-1, and MOF-177 and found that gravimetric capacities of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ varies from $3.2\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-2 to $33.5\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-177 at ambient temperature and pressure of 45 bar. Furukawa and Yaghi [16] also investigated adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in various covalent organic frameworks (COFs). At 10 bar, 273 K, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption is $4.5{-}28\mathrm{mmol/g}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption is about $2.1{-}6.2\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ Ma and Zhou [17] summarized $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption on various MOFs and the capacities at 35 bar, $298\mathrm{K}$ are $1.8{-}11.2\mathrm{mmol/g}$ . Table 3 summarizes $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption on various MOF samples. Thus, $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOFs in this investigation present reasonable adsorption capacities for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption.
Fig. 7 shows selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFNH4. The selectivity is the ratio of adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at a given pressure. As seen, the selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ decreased with increasing pressure for both samples. $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-NH4 displayed higher selectivity than Zr-MOF. For $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF, the selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is between 2.2 and 3.8, while for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-NH4 selectivity is between 2.6 and 4.3. In general, selective adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in porous materials can be attributed to either size exclusion or gas–pore interaction. In this work, FTIR could not detect $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ functional group, due to low concentration of $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ additive during preparation, and the interaction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to pores of Zr-MOF-NH4 may not stronger. Thus, we believe that higher selectivity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\big/}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in Zr-MOF-NH4 can be attributed to the change of pore size. The pore size distributions in Fig. 4 shows a shift of pore size on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-NH4, which has smaller pore size centered at 1.7 and $3.4\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The smaller pore size makes better selective adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
## 4. Conclusion
Addition of ammonium hydroxide in the synthesis process of Zr-MOF will produce some effects on structure and properties of Zr-MOFs. XRD, FTIR, and TGA analyses indicate that the crystalline structure and functionality remained unchanged while SEM and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption show that the textural properties were different. Addition of ammonium hydroxide did not sacrifice the thermal stability but resulted in reduction in surface area, pore volume, and particle size. The capacities of carbon dioxide and methane adsorption have been slightly reduced. However, the selectivity of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ was enhanced.
